Hi,

I built a desktop application for 3D printing and think two of its ideas
might interest your readers more than the application itself.

The first one is tolerance calibration. Instead of taking clearances from
a table, the user prints one small test piece once =E2=80=94 a strip of pins =
and
holes stepped in 0.05 mm increments. Whichever pair fits becomes the
clearance value for that filament, and every part built afterwards uses
it. Your printer, your filament, your number.

The second one is the boundary drawn around the language model. There is
an optional chat, and it can only trigger the same operations the menus
expose =E2=80=94 it cannot touch geometry. Every mesh operation is computed by
code against manifold3d; the model just picks operations and parameters,
and a proposal arrives as exactly one transaction that a single undo takes
back. It runs locally on your own GPU if you want it to, and the whole
application works with the AI switched off entirely.

Everything is non-destructive: operations stay in a stack, so changing a
dimension twenty steps back recomputes the rest.

Free complete demo, no account, runs offline: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Windows 10/11, around 173 MB; the macOS and Linux packages are following.

Happy to answer anything or supply images.

